} {"question" "q-31229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $g(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-7,-6)$ and $(2,5).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave down on the intervals $(-7,-6)$ and $(2,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-31229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "425d5a71c4ad064b44f858c7994519e7f6c779771f166c169d79f678eba9b755"} {"question" "q-31229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-31229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-2,2)$ and $(2,5)$"] ["text" "$(-6,-2)$ and $(-2,2)$"] ["text" "$(-6,-2)$ only"] ["text" "$(-7,-6)$ and $(2,5)$"] ["text" "$(2,5)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-31229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-7,-6)$ and $(2,5)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${x}^{2}>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{3}-1<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "425d5a71c4ad064b44f858c7994519e7f6c779771f166c169d79f678eba9b755"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114277" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2} \\\\  & =60{x}^{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{2}(x-1) & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval   $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning, this leads to the inequality $x-1<0$ with $x\\ne 0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "425d5a71c4ad064b44f858c7994519e7f6c779771f166c169d79f678eba9b755"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "425d5a71c4ad064b44f858c7994519e7f6c779771f166c169d79f678eba9b755"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-114270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-79045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=6+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-79045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & >0\\,⟹\\,x<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the function is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $2-x<0,$ which has the solution $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-79045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "425d5a71c4ad064b44f858c7994519e7f6c779771f166c169d79f678eba9b755"} {"question" "q-79045" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-79045" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-79045" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-78960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-78960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $[0,\\pi ].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0$ when $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi .$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ which has the solution $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-78960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "425d5a71c4ad064b44f858c7994519e7f6c779771f166c169d79f678eba9b755"} {"question" "q-78960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-78960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "q-78960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "032a6a43059b540d42ba578cc0fd51d00451ad0c72b6a1268551184346ad5c3b"} {"question" "e-3740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the intervals on which the function $f(x) = x^3 -3x$ is concave up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3740.json" "file_sha256" "d9fbc58d96fa43f3aee64196ad54d480022dc7e7f0c143612f949dd9110bcc6e"} {"question" "e-3740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)= x^3 -3x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & >0 \\\\ x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3740.json" "file_sha256" "d9fbc58d96fa43f3aee64196ad54d480022dc7e7f0c143612f949dd9110bcc6e"} {"question" "e-3745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph below shows $y=g''(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/a4d19390678a977117b32211d87df80f15e863fbe93d4068bab0a9bf1080cb0b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3745.json" "file_sha256" "f1492a561f40618311f1c2740a4d7ebbfbde9f2fd7ee0cacdc4f537a3c749ea2"} {"question" "e-3745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x),$ we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5).$ Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3745.json" "file_sha256" "f1492a561f40618311f1c2740a4d7ebbfbde9f2fd7ee0cacdc4f537a3c749ea2"} {"question" "e-3741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3741.json" "file_sha256" "95244f3298536c1daf9bd85d41df8b368fbd8e7b7635f0abfbcc7ab021f8febd"} {"question" "e-3741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <          0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1)  >        0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and $\\left(1,\\infty\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/example-3741.json" "file_sha256" "95244f3298536c1daf9bd85d41df8b368fbd8e7b7635f0abfbcc7ab021f8febd"}] "usage" {"q-31214" [] "q-31229" [] "e-3740" [] "e-3745" [] "e-3741" []} "basis" 1224 "retractions" [[17592186184685 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3846/Source/Images/q-31214.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y = f^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above.\nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up."] [17592186184686 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3846/Source/Images/q-31229.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y = g^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ is shown above.\nFind the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave down."] [17592186184004 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b)$.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^3 -3x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3x^{2} - 3 \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x & > 0 \\\\ x & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty)$."] [17592186184006 :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=g''(x)$, where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5]$. Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3846/Source/Images/bbbc1786039bf107ff38f347061b540d.png)"] [17592186184006 :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b)$.\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x)$, we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5)$. Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5)$."] [17592186184005 :question/problem "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4$."] [17592186184005 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 2x^{3} - x^{4} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 6x^{2} - 4x^{3} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 12x - 12x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12x - 12x^{2} & > 0 \\\\ 12x^{2} - 12x & < 0 \\\\ x(x - 1) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3846/Source/Images/b2ab07481181ff8e94caf76114a28445.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1$. So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,1)$.\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) < 0$. By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1) > 0$, which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186183005 17592186183006 17592186183007 17592186183008 17592186183009 17592186183011 17592186183012 17592186183013 17592186183014 17592186183015] "immutable_fields" [[17592186183004 :answer-field/correct] [17592186183004 :answer-field/id] [17592186183004 :answer-field/key] [17592186183004 :answer-field/type] [17592186183010 :answer-field/correct] [17592186183010 :answer-field/id] [17592186183010 :answer-field/key] [17592186183010 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
